To focus on an element found by `getElementsByTagName` in JavaScript, you can use the `focus()` method on the returned element.

Here's an example:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input"); // get all <input> elements
var focusedElement = elements[0]; // select the first element
focusedElement.focus(); // focus on the selected element
```
This will focus on the first `<input>` element found on the page.

If you want to focus on a specific element among the ones returned by `getElementsByTagName`, you can iterate over the elements and find the one you're interested in:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
for (var i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
  if (elements[i].id === "myInput") { // for example, find an element with id "myInput"
    elements[i].focus();
    break; // stop iterating once the desired element is found
  }
}
```
Make sure to check that the element actually exists before trying to focus on it, by checking the `elements.length` property:
```
var elements = document.getElementsByTagName("input");
if (elements.length > 0) {
  var focusedElement = elements[0];
  focusedElement.focus();
}
```
Keep in mind that `getElementsByTagName` returns a `HTMLCollection` object, which is a live collection of elements. This means that if you update the DOM and add or remove elements, the `HTMLCollection` will reflect those changes.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

